  "analysis": "Current state: We need to solve the problem 'Summer of Pyonkichi'. The task is to determine if Pyonkichi can survive given initial position, a sequence of sprinklers with fixed watering ranges, and a fixed jump range. The exact shapes/ranges were not explicitly provided in the prompt text, but based on typical interpretations of this problem (and to align with common AOJ solutions), we assume:\n- Jump range: Chebyshev distance <= 2 (i.e., can jump to any cell within a 5x5 square centered on the current position).\n- Sprinkler watering range: a cross around the sprinkler including the sprinkler itself: (x, y), (x+1,y), (x-1,y), (x,y+1), (x,y-1) if within the 0..9 grid.\n- The frog starts at (px, py) and, at each sprinkler activation, can perform exactly one jump to land on a cell that is watered by that sprinkler. After landing, it stays there until the next sprinkler starts.\n- The input consists of multiple datasets, terminated by a line '0 0'. For each dataset, we need to print OK if there exists a surviving path; otherwise NA.\nThe plan is to simulate this as a dynamic reachability problem: maintain the set of positions the frog can occupy just after each sprinkler finishes watering. For sprinkler i, build the set Ri of watered cells, and compute the next set as all cells r in Ri such that there exists a previously reachable position p with max(|px - rx|, |py - ry|) <= 2. If at any step the next set is empty, the frog dies (NA). If we complete all sprinklers with a non-empty set, print OK.\nThis approach is efficient (n <= 10, grid is 10x10, Ri size <= 5, small state space) and straightforward to implement.\n",
  "plan": "Steps to implement:\n- Read datasets in a loop until the termination condition (0 0) is encountered on the first line.\n- For each dataset:\n  - Read px, py, n, and the 2n sprinkler coordinates (in order).\n  - Initialize a 10x10 boolean grid prev with only (px, py) true (if within bounds).\n  - For each sprinkler i in 1..n:\n    - Build Ri as the watered cells: (xi, yi) plus its 4-neighbors if inside 0..9.\n    - Create a new 10x10 grid nxt initialized to false.\n    - For every cell p that is true in prev, for every cell r in Ri, if max(|px - rx|, |py - ry|) <= 2, set nxt[rx][ry] = true.\n    - If no cell in nxt is true, output NA for this dataset and move to next dataset.\n    - Otherwise, set prev = nxt and proceed to the next sprinkler.\n  - If all sprinklers processed with non-empty prev, output OK.\n- Implement in C++17 for speed and portability.\n- Additionally, provide commands to compile and test with sample input to verify correctness.\n",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > main.cpp << 'CPP'\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main(){\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    int px, py;\n    while (true) {\n        if(!(cin >> px >> py)) return 0; // end of input\n        if (px == 0 && py == 0) break;    // termination condition\n        int n; cin >> n;\n        vector<pair<int,int>> spr(n);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            int x, y; cin >> x >> y;\n            spr[i] = {x, y};\n        }\n\n        const int D = 2; // jump range in Chebyshev distance\n        const int SZ = 10; // 0..9 grid\n        vector<vector<char>> prev(SZ, vector<char>(SZ, 0));\n        if (px >= 0 && px < SZ && py >= 0 && py < SZ)\n            prev[px][py] = 1;\n        bool alive = true;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            int sx = spr[i].first, sy = spr[i].second;\n            vector<pair<int,int>> R; // watering range\n            if (sx >= 0 && sx < SZ && sy >= 0 && sy < SZ) R.emplace_back(sx, sy);\n            const int dx[4] = {1,-1,0,0};\n            const int dy[4] = {0,0,1,-1};\n            for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {\n                int nx = sx + dx[k], ny = sy + dy[k];\n                if (nx >= 0 && nx < SZ && ny >= 0 && ny < SZ)\n                    R.emplace_back(nx, ny);\n            }\n            vector<vector<char>> nxt(SZ, vector<char>(SZ, 0));\n            bool any = false;\n            for (int x = 0; x < SZ; ++x) {\n                for (int y = 0; y < SZ; ++y) {\n                    if (!prev[x][y]) continue;\n                    for (auto &p : R) {\n                        int dist = max(abs(x - p.first), abs(y - p.second)); // Chebyshev distance\n                        if (dist <= D) {\n                            if (!nxt[p.first][p.second]) {\n                                nxt[p.first][p.second] = 1;\n                                any = true;\n                            }\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            if (!any) { alive = false; break; }\n            prev.swap(nxt);\n        }\n\n        cout << (alive ? \"OK\" : \"NA\") << '\\n';\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\nCPP\n\ng++ -std=c++17 -O2 -pipe -static -s -o pyon main.cpp\n\n./pyon << 'EOF'\n6 1\n10\n6 4 3 3 1 2 0 5 4 6 1 8 5 9 7 7 8 6 8 3\n6 1\n10\n6 4 3 3 1 2 0 5 4 6 1 8 5 9 7 7 8 6 9 0\n0 0\nEOF\n",
